WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.562
Original Medicare leaves Dennis with the Part A deductible each

00:00:04.562 --> 00:00:09.123
benefit period, 20 percent of Part B charges with no

00:00:09.123 --> 00:00:13.229
limit, possible excess charges and nothing for emergencies abroad.

00:00:13.229 --> 00:00:19.159
Medicare supplement insurance, or Medigap, is a private policy that pays those gaps.

00:00:19.159 --> 00:00:24.177
It works only with Original Medicare: Dennis must have Parts A

00:00:24.177 --> 00:00:29.194
and B, Medicare pays first and the supplement pays second, and

00:00:29.194 --> 00:00:34.212
he may not hold one while in a Medicare Advantage plan.

00:00:34.212 --> 00:00:39.686
Each policy covers one person, so Dennis and his wife need two.

00:00:39.686 --> 00:00:45.160
Medigap does not cover prescription drugs; a Part D plan does that.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:51.333
The most important date in Medigap is the six-month open enrollment period that begins

00:00:51.333 --> 00:00:57.066
the first month Dennis is 65 or older and enrolled in Part B.

00:00:57.066 --> 00:01:02.357
During those six months any insurer selling Medigap in Maryland must sell

00:01:02.357 --> 00:01:07.649
him any plan it offers, may not refuse him or charge more

00:01:07.649 --> 00:01:12.941
because of his health, and may not delay coverage of pre-existing conditions

00:01:12.941 --> 00:01:17.350
for more than six months, reduced by prior creditable coverage.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:20.878
After the window closes, insurers may underwrite, charge

00:01:20.878 --> 00:01:23.965
more or decline, except in guaranteed-issue situations.

00:01:23.965 --> 00:01:27.492
Lesson 45 adds Maryland’s extra protections, including a

00:01:27.492 --> 00:01:31.020
birthday window and rules for people under 65.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.079
Insurers rate Medigap three ways.

00:01:33.079 --> 00:01:38.021
Attained-age rating charges a premium based on the insured’s current age, so

00:01:38.021 --> 00:01:42.962
the premium rises every year as Dennis gets older, on top of

00:01:42.962 --> 00:01:47.492
inflation increases; it is cheapest at 65 and most expensive later.

00:01:47.492 --> 00:01:51.610
Issue-age rating sets the premium by the age at purchase;

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.316
it rises only for inflation, never because Dennis aged.

00:01:55.316 --> 00:02:00.670
Community rating charges everyone the same premium regardless of age, rising only for

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:06.023
inflation; it is highest for the youngest buyers and fairest to the oldest.

00:02:06.023 --> 00:02:09.317
Theo shows Dennis the three price paths over

00:02:09.317 --> 00:02:12.200
twenty years, not just the first-year quote.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.861
Federal law standardizes Medigap into lettered plans, A through N, so

00:02:16.861 --> 00:02:21.521
that a Plan G from one insurer is identical to a

00:02:21.521 --> 00:02:25.335
Plan G from another; only price and service differ.

00:02:25.335 --> 00:02:30.419
Every plan includes the core benefits: Part A hospital coinsurance for days

00:02:30.419 --> 00:02:35.503
61 through 90 and the lifetime reserve days, plus 365 additional hospital

00:02:35.503 --> 00:02:40.588
days after Medicare benefits end; Part B coinsurance or copayments; the first

00:02:40.588 --> 00:02:44.401
three pints of blood; and Part A hospice coinsurance.

00:02:44.401 --> 00:02:47.367
Plan A offers only the core benefits.

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:51.180
Every insurer that sells Medigap must offer Plan A.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.916
The richer letters add benefits: the skilled nursing facility coinsurance for days 21

00:02:55.916 --> 00:03:00.651
through 100, the Part A deductible, Part B excess charges, and emergency care

00:03:00.651 --> 00:03:04.658
during foreign travel at 80 percent up to a lifetime limit.

00:03:04.658 --> 00:03:09.394
Plans C and F also pay the Part B deductible, but federal law

00:03:09.394 --> 00:03:14.129
closed them to anyone who became eligible for Medicare on or after January

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:18.865
1, 2020, which made Plan G the most comprehensive plan for new enrollees.

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:21.779
Plans K and L pay a percentage of

00:03:21.779 --> 00:03:24.693
some benefits in exchange for an out-of-pocket limit.

00:03:24.693 --> 00:03:29.429
High-deductible versions of Plans F and G pay nothing until the insured meets

00:03:29.429 --> 00:03:33.800
an annual deductible, indexed yearly, in exchange for a much lower premium.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.256
Dennis enrolls in Part B in March and shops in his six-month window.

00:03:38.256 --> 00:03:40.998
Plan G from three insurers covers the same

00:03:40.998 --> 00:03:43.398
things: everything but the Part B deductible.

00:03:43.398 --> 00:03:48.197
One insurer uses attained-age rating and is cheapest now; one uses issue-age and costs

00:03:48.197 --> 00:03:52.653
more now but will not rise with his age; one is community rated.

00:03:52.653 --> 00:03:55.738
He asks about high-deductible G as a lower-cost option.

00:03:55.738 --> 00:04:00.537
Because he is in open enrollment, none of them may ask about his health.

00:04:00.537 --> 00:04:03.964
Theo documents the comparison, explains that Plan G plus a

00:04:03.964 --> 00:04:07.392
Part D plan completes his coverage, and reminds him that

00:04:07.392 --> 00:04:10.820
the policy carries a 30-day right to return in Maryland.
